    <dashboard enable-sort-zone-taborder='true' name='Dashboard Nov 2010 Cohort: A Zoom-In'>
      <style />
      <size maxheight='800' maxwidth='1000' minheight='800' minwidth='1000' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='50000' id='6' type-v2='text' w='91100' x='2200' y='11375'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>Every one of the following six worksheets shows the same customer population — 618 customers who first purchased between October and December 2010 — cut six different ways: acquisition month, frequency buckets, high-value tail, last-order timing, lifecycle, and isolation from the broader dataset.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>None of the six bars are meant to be the finding. </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>The bar is the door, not the room.</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'> Every one of them is deliberately unremarkable on its own — flat, ordinary-looking, nothing that jumps out. The real substance only shows up once you click through to the 3D exhibit behind it.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>Take </run>
            <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>Isolated</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>, first below, as the clearest example. The bar itself shows nothing worth a second look. But open the exhibit, toggle off the 580-customer comparison population in the legend, and the cohort&apos;s actual shape appears — recency, frequency, and monetary value, isolated from everyone surrounding it. That single toggle is the entire point of this dashboard: every bar here hides a population worth looking at directly, not just counting.</run>
            <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='14'>Click any of the six that follow the same way you did in Chapter Four — each opens its own 3D exhibit, in a new tab, free to rotate and filter. </run>
          </formatted-text>
        </zone>
        <zone forceUpdate='true' h='9750' id='7' type-v2='text' w='92400' x='2200' y='2125'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='1' fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='24'>Nov 2010 Cohort: A Zoom-In</run>
